0:{"rsc":["$","$1","c",{"children":[[["$","$L2","5.6",{"topic":{"id":"5.6","title":"State-Sponsored Cyber Attacks","minutes":13,"summary":"Study advanced operations through intrusion paths, supply-chain trust, and carefully qualified attribution.","sections":[{"title":"Why advanced operations are different","body":["State-sponsored or state-linked operations may combine substantial resources, long-term access, custom tooling, and careful operational security. Yet sophistication is not a reliable attribution test by itself. Criminal groups can reuse advanced tools, and state-linked operators can use commodity malware. Define the observed capabilities rather than inferring sponsorship from complexity.","The SolarWinds Orion compromise illustrates the risk of trusted software updates and supply-chain access. A compromised build or update path can reach many organizations, but the downstream impact differs by victim and follow-on activity. Distinguish initial compromise, distribution, access, lateral movement, and actual data or service effects."]},{"title":"Intrusion reconstruction","body":["Build a timeline from build-system records, update distribution, authentication events, endpoint telemetry, and network observations. Identify the trust relationship that enabled access and the controls that failed or succeeded. ATT&CK can organize techniques such as valid accounts or lateral movement, but each mapping needs supporting observations.","Check for alternative explanations and gaps. A missing log does not prove the attacker deleted it; retention or configuration may explain the absence. Preserve relevant telemetry before rotation and protect credentials and tokens that may still enable access. Avoid publishing sensitive victim details in an attribution exercise."]},{"title":"Attribution and response","body":["Technical clustering can show shared infrastructure or code, while intelligence may connect an operation to an organization. Political attribution adds another layer of judgment. Report each level separately and state confidence. A tool signature associated with a group in public reporting is not proof that the group conducted every matching incident.","Response may involve coordinated containment, credential rotation, trusted rebuilds, provider action, diplomatic measures, or law enforcement. The chosen response depends on authority, evidence, and operational risk. National-security objectives do not remove the need for reliable facts, proportionate action, and clear accountability."]}],"practice":{"title":"Trace a trusted-update incident","scenario":"A fictional software update is followed by unusual authentication and new cloud roles.","steps":["Separate the update event from later account activity.","Identify the records needed to connect the stages.","Write technical and organizational attribution judgments separately.","Recommend evidence-preserving containment and a confidence review."],"debrief":"A supply-chain investigation follows trust relationships and event sequences. It should not jump from a suspicious update to a named state without evidence."},"terms":[],"lawIds":["it-act","bnss"],"sources":["solarwinds","mitre","first"],"labId":"network","unit":5,"objectives":["Explain why advanced operations are different and apply it to an investigation.","Explain intrusion reconstruction and apply it to an investigation.","Explain attribution and response and apply it to an investigation."],"question":{"id":"q-5.6","prompt":"Why is malware reuse alone weak attribution evidence?","options":["Malware cannot contain identifying information.","Tools can be shared, copied, leaked, or deliberately reused as false flags.","Every criminal group writes unique code.","Reuse proves state sponsorship automatically."],"answer":1,"explanation":"Shared code is one contextual clue and must be evaluated with other technical and intelligence evidence.","unit":5}}}]],["$L3"],"$L4"]}],"isPartial":false,"staleTime":300,"varyParams":null,"buildId":"mMiCr23A_-Cpk0hZkfT7X"}
